r' to pack them together. Must be one of: 'center', 'end', 'spaceAround', 'spaceBetween', 'spaceEvenly', 'start', 'stretch'\n  - :align: Defines the alignment of children along the cross axis (vertically). This is similar to the CSS 'align-items' property, but uses camelCase values (e.g., 'start'). Must be one of: 'start', 'center', 'end', 'stretch'\n  - :weight: The relative weight of this component within a Row or Column. This is similar to the CSS 'flex-grow' property. Note: this may ONLY be set when the component is a direct descendant of a Row or Column.\n- (Slider :label? :min? :max :value :steps? :weight? :checks?)\n  - :label: The label for the slider.\n  - :min: The minimum value of the slider.\n  - :max: The maximum value of the slider.\n  - :value: The current value of the slider.\n  - :steps: The number of discrete divisions in the slider range. If specified, the slider will snap to discrete values.\n  - :weight: The relative weight of this component within a Row or Column. This is similar to the CSS 'flex-grow' property. Note: this may ONLY be set when the component is a direct descendant of a Row or Column.\n- (Tabs :tabs :weight?)\n  - :tabs: An array of objects, where each object defines a tab with a title and a child component.\n  - :weight: The relative weight of this component within a Row or Column. This is similar to the CSS 'flex-grow' property. Note: this may ONLY be set when the component is a direct descendant of a Row or Column.\n- (Text :text :variant? :weight?)\n  - :text: The text content to display. While simple Markdown formatting is supported (i.e. without HTML, images, or links), utilizing dedicated UI components is generally preferred for a richer and more structured presentation.\n  - :variant: A hint for the base text style. Must be one of: 'caption', 'body'\n  - :weight: The relative weight of this component within a Row or Column. This is similar to the CSS 'flex-grow' property. Note: this may ONLY be set when the component is a direct descendant of a Row or Column.\n- (TextField :label :value? :placeholder? :variant? :weight? :checks?)\n  - :label: The text label for the input field.\n  - :value: The value of the text field.\n  - :placeholder: The placeholder text for the input field.\n  - :variant: The type of input field to display. Must be one of: 'longText', 'number', 'shortText', 'obscured'\n  - :weight: The relative weight of this component within a Row or Column. This is similar to the CSS 'flex-grow' property. Note: this may ONLY be set when the component is a direct descendant of a Row or Column.\n- (Video :url :posterUrl? :weight?)\n  - :url: The URL of the video to display.\n  - :posterUrl: The URL of the poster image to display before the video plays.\n  - :weight: The relative weight of this component within a Row or Column. This is similar to the CSS 'flex-grow' property. Note: this may ONLY be set when the component is a direct descendant of a Row or Column.\n\n## Function Signatures:\n- (and :values)\n  - Performs a logical AND operation on a list of boolean values.\n- (email :value)\n  - Checks that the value is a valid email address.\n- (formatCurrency :value :currency :decimals? :grouping?)\n  - Formats a number as a currency string.\n- (formatDate :value :format)\n  - Formats a timestamp into a string using a pattern.\n- (formatNumber :value :decimals? :grouping?)\n  - Formats a number with the specified grouping and decimal precision.\n- (formatString :value)\n  - Performs string interpolation of data model values and other functions in the catalog functions list and returns the resulting string. The value string can contain interpolated expressions in the `${expression}` format. Supported expression types include: JSON Pointer paths to the data model (e.g., `${/absolute/path}` or `${relative/path}`), and client-side function calls (e.g., `${now()}`). Function arguments must be named (e.g., `${formatDate(value:${/currentDate}, format:'MM-dd')}`). To include a literal `${` sequence, escape it as `\\${`.\n- (length :value :min? :max?)\n  - Checks string length constraints.\n- (not :value)\n  - Performs a logical NOT operation on a boolean value.\n- (numeric :value :min? :max?)\n  - Checks numeric range constraints.\n- (openUrl :url)\n  - Opens the specified URL in a browser or handler. This function has no return value.\n- (or :values)\n  - Performs a logical OR operation on a list of boolean values.\n- (pluralize :value :zero? :one? :two? :few? :many? :other)\n  - Returns a localized string based on the Common Locale Data Repository (CLDR) plural category of the count (zero, one, two, few, many, other). Requires an 'other' fallback. For English, just use 'one' and 'other'.\n- (regex :value :pattern)\n  - Checks that the value matches a regular expression string.\n- (required :value)\n  - Checks that the value is not null, undefined, or empty.",
+        "8639e680b6b11f5b3967e45f7d1cd459": "**My UI Design Journey**\n\nOkay, here's the mental path I took to design this UI, as if I'm walking through it myself. The goal is a UI on the `main` surface, a vertical list of two sections: Dog Breed Info and Dog Generator.\n\nFirst things first, let's nail down the structure. I know there needs to be a single root component with the ID `root` (even though that isn't always explicitly in the signatures). Looking at the catalog, `Column` seems perfect as the root – it's a container and handles vertical layout. It will be the root, and I'll add the `:id \"root\"` to it. Now I need to decide what to put in that column.\n\nI need two sections. Looking at my knowledge of the available components, a `Card` seems perfect to separate the content, so I'll nest two `Card` components inside the root `Column`.\n\nI see from the instructions that the components need to be single root components, and that `:child` in the Card component should just be the ID. But that's where I have to remember the A2UI Atom output contract says to nest the children directly in the parent. So I will nest a `Column` directly inside each `Card` component, and those `Columns` will hold the individual card content.\n\nData is important, so I will initialize the data, like a typical programming design. I will set up initial data for breeds, the form, and the skills list using a `data` block and set it in the root `Column`.\n\nLet's do the \"Dog breed information\" Card. It needs a title, header image, and a horizontal list of dog breed images.\n\nI decided that for the title I'll use a `Text` component and will use the text content \"**Famous Dog Breeds**\" with a variant. I also need an `Image` for the header, so I will get the information from the data. The next step is a horizontal list, the `List` component is perfect here. For the list items, I'll use a `template` to dynamically render each dog breed. The `template` will contain a nested `Column` with an `Image` and a `Text` element displaying the breed name. To nest components, I will follow the A2UI Atom pattern and nest the children inside the container elements.\n\nMoving onto the \"Dog Generator\" Card:\n\nI need a title for the section, using another `Text` component. I need a descriptive section and I can use the same technique. Now for the input, I need text fields for breed name and number of legs, using the `TextField` component and set them to the form. A `ChoicePicker` for the dog skills will allow the user to select multiple skills. And then the magic - a `Button` to generate the breed. I will also have an event bound to it for generating the breed name and skills. I will pass the information from the form to the backend through the event and the backend will generate the breed.\n\nThe last part is a divider to visually separate the input form with another `Column` where I will display the result, which I'll format as a live preview. Using the `formatString` function to bind the `TextField` data to the text component.\n\nFinally, I carefully verify the properties of each component used, ensuring they all match the specifications in the provided catalog and that I'm sticking to the A2UI Atom S-Expression format – nesting components directly, using colons for properties, and handling data correctly. This will make the UI both correct and compliant! The whole thing is wrapped in `<a2ui>` tags. I am now confident that my A2UI Atom S-Expression is fully compliant.\n\n\n",
